Title: The Velveteen Rabbit

Credit: Written by

Author: Margery Williams

Source: Adapted from book

Draft date: 7/30/2026

FADE IN:

INT. NURSERY - DAY

A hand-crafted period storybook world rendered in warm, textured illustration: people, toys, and animals share the same medium.

Christmas morning. VELVETEEN RABBIT, a small stuffed rabbit with a fat, bunchy body, brown-and-white spotted velveteen, pink sateen-lined ears, thread whiskers, and black boot-button eyes, sits wedged in the top of the BOY'S stocking with a sprig of holly between his paws.

The BOY, six, bright-eyed and tousle-haired, pulls him free and hugs him close.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

For at least two hours the Boy loved him.

Tissue paper rustles. Aunts and Uncles arrive with new presents. The Boy rushes from toy to toy, and the Velveteen Rabbit is forgotten among the parcels.

INT. NURSERY - DAY

Long afterward, the Rabbit lives in the toy cupboard and on the nursery floor. Mechanical toys with polished metal bodies turn away from him. A model boat displays its peeling rigging. A jointed wooden lion sits stiffly apart.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

The mechanical toys were very superior, and looked down upon every one else. The Rabbit could not claim to be a model of anything.

Only the SKIN HORSE stays beside him. He is an old brown toy with bald patches, exposed seams, and a tail missing most of its hairs.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

The Skin Horse had lived longer in the nursery than any of the others. He had seen mechanical toys arrive to boast and swagger, and by-and-by break their mainsprings and pass away.

INT. NURSERY - NIGHT

Near the nursery fender, the Velveteen Rabbit and the Skin Horse lie side by side in the moonlight, waiting for NANA to tidy the room.

VELVETEEN RABBIT

What is REAL? Does it mean having things that buzz inside you and a stick-out handle?

SKIN HORSE

Real isn't how you are made. It's a thing that happens to you. When a child loves you for a long, long time, not just to play with, but REALLY loves you, then you become Real.

VELVETEEN RABBIT

Does it hurt?

SKIN HORSE

Sometimes. When you are Real you don't mind being hurt.

VELVETEEN RABBIT

Does it happen all at once, like being wound up, or bit by bit?

SKIN HORSE

It doesn't happen all at once. You become. It takes a long time. That's why it doesn't happen often to people who break easily, or have sharp edges, or who have to be carefully kept.

SKIN HORSE

Generally, by the time you are Real, most of your hair has been loved off, and your eyes drop out and you get loose in the joints and very shabby. But these things don't matter at all, because once you are Real you can't be ugly, except to people who don't understand.

VELVETEEN RABBIT

I suppose you are real?

The Skin Horse only smiles.

SKIN HORSE

The Boy's Uncle made me Real. That was a great many years ago; but once you are Real you can't become unreal again. It lasts for always.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

The Rabbit sighed. He thought it would be a long time before this magic called Real happened to him.

INT. NURSERY - NIGHT

Bedtime. The BOY searches for the china dog that usually sleeps with him. NANA, a brisk older nursemaid in a white apron, looks around the room and spots the open toy cupboard.

NANA

Here, take your old Bunny! He'll do to sleep with you!

She drags the Rabbit out by one ear and places him in the Boy's arms.

Night after night, the Boy hugs him tight, rolls over on him, and pushes him beneath the pillow. He also makes tunnels under the bedclothes and calls them the burrows real rabbits live in.

When Nana leaves for supper, Boy and Rabbit play splendid games in whispers beside the burning night-light. At last, the Boy's hands clasp close around the Rabbit under his chin.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

And so time went on, and the little Rabbit was very happy.

The Rabbit's beautiful velveteen fur grows shabbier. His tail becomes unsewn. The pink rubs away from his nose where the Boy kisses him.

EXT. GARDEN - DAY

Spring. The Boy pushes the Velveteen Rabbit in a wheelbarrow, spreads a picnic on the grass, and builds him fairy huts beneath the raspberry canes.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

Spring came, and they had long days in the garden, for wherever the Boy went the Rabbit went too.

The Boy is called away suddenly to tea, leaving Rabbit on the lawn. The light fades.

EXT. GARDEN - NIGHT

Long after dusk, NANA searches the garden by candlelight. She finds the Rabbit soaked with dew and caked with earth from the flower-bed burrows.

She rubs him with a corner of her apron.

NANA

You must have your old Bunny! Fancy all that fuss for a toy!

INT. NURSERY - NIGHT

The Boy sits up in bed and stretches out his hands as Nana brings the Rabbit in.

BOY

Give me my Bunny! You mustn't say that. He isn't a toy. He's REAL!

The Rabbit rests against the Boy's chest. His worn button eyes seem suddenly wise.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

The nursery magic had happened to him, and he was a toy no longer. He was Real.

INT. NURSERY - DAY

The next morning, NANA picks up the shabby Rabbit. She studies his faded spots and his knowing black eyes.

NANA

I declare if that old Bunny hasn't got quite a knowing expression!

EXT. WOOD - DAY

Summer. In the long June light, the Boy makes the Rabbit a nest among the bracken, then wanders away to pick flowers.

Two wild rabbits creep out of the tall grass. They are furry and brand-new, with hidden seams, twitching ears, and bodies that change shape as they move.

WILD RABBIT

Why don't you get up and play with us?

VELVETEEN RABBIT

I don't feel like it.

The WILD RABBIT gives a big sideways hop and stands on his hind legs.

WILD RABBIT

Ho! It's as easy as anything. I don't believe you can!

VELVETEEN RABBIT

I can! I can jump higher than anything!

WILD RABBIT

Can you hop on your hind legs?

The Rabbit sits very still, his back made all in one piece.

VELVETEEN RABBIT

I don't want to!

The WILD RABBIT stretches out his neck and looks him over.

WILD RABBIT

He hasn't got any hind legs! Fancy a rabbit without any hind legs!

The wild rabbit laughs.

VELVETEEN RABBIT

I have! I am sitting on them!

WILD RABBIT

Then stretch them out and show me, like this!

He whirls round and dances. The Rabbit watches, dizzy with longing.

VELVETEEN RABBIT

I don't like dancing. I'd rather sit still!

The WILD RABBIT comes close and sniffs him. His ears flatten. He jumps backward.

WILD RABBIT

He doesn't smell right! He isn't a rabbit at all! He isn't real!

VELVETEEN RABBIT

I am Real! I am Real! The Boy said so!

Footsteps approach. The Boy runs past. With a stamp of feet and a flash of white tails, the wild rabbits vanish into the bracken.

VELVETEEN RABBIT

Come back and play with me! Oh, do come back! I know I am Real!

Only ants move between his paws. The bracken sways. The Rabbit lies alone until the Boy returns and carries him home.

INT. NURSERY - NIGHT

Weeks pass. The Rabbit's whiskers are loved off, his ear lining has turned gray, his brown spots have faded, and his shape is nearly gone.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

Weeks passed, and the little Rabbit grew very old and shabby, but the Boy loved him just as much.

The Boy's face grows flushed. He talks in his sleep. His body is so hot that it burns the Rabbit when he holds him.

A lamp burns through the night. Shadows of care pass over the bed. Hidden beneath the bedclothes, the Rabbit never stirs.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

He knew that the Boy needed him.

The sickroom drags through another night. The Boy's hand searches weakly beneath the covers. The Rabbit presses close under his chin.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

It was a long weary time.

The fever turns. The Boy's breathing eases, and his hand closes around the Rabbit.

INT. NURSERY - DAY

A bright morning. The windows stand wide open. The Boy sits outside on the balcony, wrapped in a shawl, while the Rabbit lies tangled among the bedclothes with only his head peeping out.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

The Boy was going to the seaside to-morrow.

The Doctor examines the room. A suitcase waits nearby. Nana gathers the picture-books and toys that were played with during the illness.

NANA

How about his old Bunny?

DOCTOR

That? Why, it's a mass of scarlet fever germs! Burn it at once. What? Nonsense! Get him a new one. He mustn't have that any more!

The Rabbit hears the word burn. Beyond the open window, he imagines great waves, tiny crabs, and sand castles.

INT. BOY'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

In a different bedroom, the Boy sleeps with a new bunny made of white plush, its glass eyes shining.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

He was too excited to care very much about the new bunny. To-morrow he was going to the seaside.

EXT. GARDEN BEHIND FOWL-HOUSE - DAY

At the end of the garden, a sack lies beside the fowl-house. Inside it are the old picture-books, rubbish, and the Velveteen Rabbit.

No fire is lit. A spade, potato basket, and gathered peas show why. The gardener's work goes on elsewhere.

The sack is left untied.

EXT. GARDEN BEHIND FOWL-HOUSE - NIGHT

Moonlight washes the abandoned sack. The Rabbit wriggles his head through the opening.

His coat has worn so thin from years of hugging that it gives him no warmth. Nearby, the raspberry canes rise like a jungle.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

He seemed to see the long sunlit hours pass before him: the fairy huts in the flower-bed, the quiet evenings in the wood, and the wonderful day when he first knew that he was Real.

The Rabbit looks toward the dark garden.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

Of what use was it to be loved and lose one's beauty and become Real if it all ended like this?

A tear -- a real tear -- trickles down his shabby velvet nose and falls to the ground.

Where it lands, a strange flower grows. Slender emerald leaves unfold around a blossom like a golden cup.

The blossom opens. FAIRY steps out, the loveliest fairy in the world, dressed in pearl and dewdrops with flowers at her neck and in her hair.

She gathers the Rabbit into her arms and kisses his damp nose.

FAIRY

Little Rabbit, don't you know who I am?

The Rabbit studies her face.

FAIRY

I am the nursery magic Fairy. I take care of all the playthings that the children have loved. When they are old and worn out and the children don't need them any more, then I come and take them away with me and turn them into Real.

VELVETEEN RABBIT

Wasn't I Real before?

FAIRY

You were Real to the Boy, because he loved you. Now you shall be Real to every one.

The Fairy rises with the Rabbit in her arms and flies toward the moonlit wood.

EXT. WOOD - NIGHT

The moon has risen. Bracken fronds shine like frosted silver.

In an open glade, wild rabbits dance with their shadows. They stop when the Fairy arrives and form a ring around her.

FAIRY

I've brought you a new playfellow. You must be very kind to him and teach him all he needs to know in Rabbit-land, for he is going to live with you for ever and ever!

She kisses the Rabbit again and sets him on the grass.

FAIRY

Run and play, little Rabbit!

The Rabbit sits still, remembering his one-piece back and fearing the wild rabbits will see it.

Something tickles his nose. He lifts a hind toe to scratch it -- and freezes in astonishment.

The dingy velveteen is gone. Brown fur, soft and shining, covers his body. His ears twitch by themselves. Long whiskers brush the grass.

He gives one leap, then springs sideways and whirls round with the other rabbits.

When he stops, the Fairy is gone.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

He was a Real Rabbit at last, at home with the other rabbits.

EXT. WOOD - DAY

Autumn passes. Winter passes. In spring, warm sunlight fills the wood.

The BOY plays among the trees. Two rabbits creep from the bracken to watch him. One is brown all over. The other has strange markings beneath his fur, as though he was once spotted. His soft nose and round black eyes are familiar.

BOY

Why, he looks just like my old Bunny that was lost when I had scarlet fever!

The spotted rabbit holds the Boy's gaze. The Boy turns back to his game.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

The Boy never knew that it really was his own Bunny, come back to look at the child who had first helped him to be Real.

The Real Rabbit bounds after the other rabbit into the sunlit bracken.

FADE OUT.

THE END
